#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98bd56e8-77ca-553a-9c26-04feae0618dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "adabb3ab-fc9c-5857-95ac-008bc560b257" :question/math-academy-id "e-8203" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(2,2^2),$ compute $P(X <               1.24).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3156 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3121 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3936 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3897 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3859 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X <       x) = P \\left( Z <       \\dfrac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 2$ and $\\sigma = 2.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.24) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.24-2}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.38) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.38) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.38)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/48d92806543cb86b2906032b1723fa942db842e7f283681cafeeb9b11fca8cab.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.38) = 0.3520.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X <               1.24) = 0.3520.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b6aec2d-db43-5ff5-bd1f-1d5b38bde7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "dad97320-cddd-501a-81bd-371090cc5be5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8204" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y \\sim N(5,4^2),$ compute $P(Y \\geq 2.92).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2743 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3085 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3050 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3015 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y \\geq y) = P \\left( Z \\geq \\dfrac{y-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 5$ and $\\sigma = 4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 2.92) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{2.92-5}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -0.52)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/049e4733082674668ebbe2c5a710cd847f1db16d26fdcd2af6e603941efdffe7.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.52) & =1-P(Z<-0.52) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.52) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.52)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.52) =0.3015.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.52) & =1-Φ(-0.52) \\\\  & =1-0.3015 \\\\  & =0.6985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y \\geq 2.92) = 0.6985.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "ab98835e-cf10-5fd3-b42b-fa50d8d95967" :question/math-academy-id "e-8205" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(1,9),$ compute $P(-1.13 <  X <       3.25).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1841 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1814 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1788 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2119 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1841 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1814 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1788 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2119 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2420 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a <        X <        b) = P \\left( \\dfrac{a-\\mu}{\\sigma} <        Z <        \\dfrac{b-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu = 1$ and $\\sigma = \\sqrt 9 = 3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.13<X<3.25) & =P(\\frac{-1.13-1}{3}<Z<\\frac{3.25-1}{3}) \\\\  & =P(-0.71<Z<0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/a50513f550d8a3122e2f2276883a0189fe9bdc6dd19159e8ae94dbd5b247f5a9.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.71<Z<0.75) & =P(Z<0.75)-P(Z\\le -0.71) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.75)-P(Z\\le -0.71) \\\\  & =Φ(0.75)-Φ(-0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.71) & =0.2389 \\\\ Φ(0.75) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.71<Z<0.75) & =Φ(0.75)-Φ(-0.71) \\\\  & =0.7734-0.2389 \\\\  & =0.5345.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-1.13<          X <          3.25) = 0.5345.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98bd56e8-77ca-553a-9c26-04feae0618dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058a186d-fc9c-581f-9ee5-46b8eeedb7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1230" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365584} {:answer/id #uuid "c2685d1d-9220-5c92-89b7-0e412180e7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365585} {:answer/id #uuid "e23c75b7-505d-5a93-b932-552fb7b1c67c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1711" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365586} {:answer/id #uuid "0e81fed1-8798-504e-ab2f-76c1603ca300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365587} {:answer/id #uuid "f2db480f-4027-5769-be22-026c2780ea0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1469" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2685d1d-9220-5c92-89b7-0e412180e7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365585} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e4f8b9b-3d2d-5c28-a5e1-96603821e8fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eedc1079-d744-5264-956d-2ac3d0d98713" :question/math-academy-id "q-131541" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{10}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<-11.7).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ | $\\,0.1230\\,$ | $\\,0.1210\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1469\\,$ | $\\,0.1446\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1711\\,$ | $\\,0.1685\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =0$ and $\\sigma =10.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-11.7) & =P(Z<\\frac{-11.7-0}{10}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.17) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.17) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/9b47010de67e748c75465b0ad1ec54d984ec7eaac6721ae9fb9b11cb60c16b16.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.17)=0.1210.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<-11.7)=0.1210.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b6aec2d-db43-5ff5-bd1f-1d5b38bde7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e94604d4-6622-556c-b24e-e75d3a9e3ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0202" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365591} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a008fc-7844-5c22-88dc-f61c34b78d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0158" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365592} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0dc4ed-057e-5986-a368-98f53a455331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365593} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6e2357-10ba-5cfd-b8f7-7b339ecb008a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0162" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365594} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0d96cb-c580-5171-b4f9-caaad7f60330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee0dc4ed-057e-5986-a368-98f53a455331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365593} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4dd89ef-f1f2-5dc1-a2f4-726df180d168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d84bcbe-db12-5f4f-9193-c0145f2e542f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131676" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(0,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 12.18).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ | $\\,0.9875\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =0$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 12.18) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{12.18-0}{6}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 2.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/c7af386057d2fba19697c0ad87c93a1865c69efaa1a5134f50bff6c7b9143dbe.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 2.03) & =1-P(Z<2.03) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 2.03) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.03)=0.9788.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 2.03) & =1-Φ(2.03) \\\\  & =1-0.9788 \\\\  & =0.0212.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 12.18)=0.0212.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b6aec2d-db43-5ff5-bd1f-1d5b38bde7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cd4ac18-1cc2-54b8-b33c-64ba113ba71d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365598} {:answer/id #uuid "1d14fcbe-0f00-57ef-8441-1db9cda2a6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d14fcbe-0f00-57ef-8441-1db9cda2a6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365599} :answer-field/id #uuid "c258a505-630b-5534-9409-2e6656dec7d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da610fe0-a3fa-57b6-9362-1c9c065a55da" :question/math-academy-id "q-276365" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(-1,{2}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 1).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-1$ and $\\sigma =2.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-(-1)}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/4cdc40bd57d5f855afa08ee21b3bb9eeeefca8c09bd4d857cf3f3b2514693110.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 1) & =1-P(Z<1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 1) & =1-Φ(1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=0.1587.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98bd56e8-77ca-553a-9c26-04feae0618dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fade9da3-6839-5bee-8e8f-b7b7dca2365a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9861" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fade9da3-6839-5bee-8e8f-b7b7dca2365a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9861" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365602} :answer-field/id #uuid "84559470-63b9-5bc2-b828-9ef9d91e350b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7729064-d321-5d9e-a76a-26a9ede41654" :question/math-academy-id "q-276366" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-2,{5}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<9).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<9)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-2$ and $\\sigma =5.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & =P(Z<\\frac{9-(-2)}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.2) \\\\  & =Φ(2.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/8ac4c36c58d5189e2483a71e2b0dbbe8e629dd9537d90b2338000d36915880a1.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(2.2)=0.9861.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<9)=0.9861.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98bd56e8-77ca-553a-9c26-04feae0618dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c51da754-fb65-5307-b8b5-7418b854969f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c51da754-fb65-5307-b8b5-7418b854969f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365605} :answer-field/id #uuid "c03948c0-45d6-5ef0-abbc-dcff80484059" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34971156-0bc6-5373-b0c9-e81344239099" :question/math-academy-id "q-276764" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(5,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<2).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2743\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3085\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3446\\,$ | $\\,0.3409\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2) & =P(Z<\\frac{2-5}{6}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.5) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/6d13179aa11fed4255ce0997c71063a6398fdbb9c6124a4048c9a29759317ab8.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.5)=0.3085.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<2)=0.3085.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b6aec2d-db43-5ff5-bd1f-1d5b38bde7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6702a4f1-c80b-5990-802c-74c71cf99d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2389" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365608} {:answer/id #uuid "91c68de4-2afe-5036-8220-d4677d5e3c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7611" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6702a4f1-c80b-5990-802c-74c71cf99d30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2389" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365608} :answer-field/id #uuid "579ff62e-f504-5131-9107-4990f8c49154" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae2d7f53-2e87-5545-b97d-8fe84a955bc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335848" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(5,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 7.84).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 7.84)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 7.84) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{7.84-5}{4}) \\\\  & =P\\,(Z\\ge \\frac{2.84}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/a5bf63797840ba4b457ef4f8564b979e106fa916b2247937733157830d6f443d.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.71) & =1-P(Z<0.71) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.71) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.71)=0.7611.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.71) & =1-0.7611 \\\\  & =0.2389.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 7.84)=0.2389.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b6aec2d-db43-5ff5-bd1f-1d5b38bde7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbfd2ad1-6682-5512-a346-8257ae277762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7324" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbfd2ad1-6682-5512-a346-8257ae277762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7324" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365612} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8cb90e3-21ba-5b4f-87f8-dec937940ca2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37ce9b44-7ef1-5943-b6f5-144d0edbcd47" :question/math-academy-id "q-335886" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(-2,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge -4.48).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2743\\,$ | $\\,0.2709\\,$ | $\\,0.2676\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.3085\\,$ | $\\,0.3050\\,$ | $\\,0.3015\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge -4.48)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-2$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.48) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.48-(-2)}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -0.62).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/2547a2e847c503d8cdf6c2df258edd7c51e1478185f2cb0055f72425ed6fd04e.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.62) & =1-P(Z<-0.62) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.62) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.62).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.62)=0.2676.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -0.62) & =1-Φ(-0.62) \\\\  & =1-0.2676 \\\\  & =0.7324.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.48)=0.7324.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b6aec2d-db43-5ff5-bd1f-1d5b38bde7e6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f53296-d904-5665-b40e-c00401698cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9793" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f53296-d904-5665-b40e-c00401698cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9793" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365615} :answer-field/id #uuid "847b2a96-eea6-5868-be71-29779072a33b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58af10d2-2fad-513b-954f-3311d42d9dfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335887" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y∼N(8,{3}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 1.88).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0166\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0207\\,$ | $\\,0.0202\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 1.88)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =8$ and $\\sigma =3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1.88) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1.88-8}{3}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -2.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/c12f91dda20714f4b97a061a52d760249c0f9d9ce5029343af3e4c216981f43d.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.04) & =1-P(Z<-2.04) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -2.04) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.04)=0.0207.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.04) & =1-Φ(-2.04) \\\\  & =1-0.0207 \\\\  & =0.9793.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1.88)=0.9793.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f15dc623-f1d0-5a74-85fe-9f0eafdb4ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9808" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365618} {:answer/id #uuid "ac92124e-ce12-5414-ac20-9223e965a3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac92124e-ce12-5414-ac20-9223e965a3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365619} :answer-field/id #uuid "11afc10f-a0ba-5218-b41b-fca307cd4a60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d44ec38-49c3-5035-8f07-fc8205dd1552" :question/math-academy-id "q-336847" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(10,{4}^{2}),$ compute $P(10.64<X<18.28).$ Use the tables below and do not round your answer.\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ | $\\,0.9803\\,$ | $\\,0.9808\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ | $\\,0.9846\\,$ | $\\,0.9850\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,0.9881\\,$ | $\\,0.9884\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ | $\\,0.9803\\,$ | $\\,0.9808\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ | $\\,0.9846\\,$ | $\\,0.9850\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,0.9881\\,$ | $\\,0.9884\\,$ |\n\n$P(10.64<X<18.28)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =10$ and $\\sigma =4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10.64<X<18.28) & =P(\\frac{10.64-10}{4}<Z<\\frac{18.28-10}{4}) \\\\  & =P(0.16<Z<2.07).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/dc1c1e8b01372821c4ad13bfb1bbfd2a102bb9d247815ace25219d3a3f42c8c2.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.16<Z<2.07) & =P(Z<2.07)-P(Z\\le 0.16) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.07)-P(Z\\le 0.16) \\\\  & =Φ(2.07)-Φ(0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.07) & =0.9808 \\\\ Φ(0.16) & =0.5636\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.16<Z<2.07) & =Φ(2.07)-Φ(0.16) \\\\  & =0.9808-0.5636 \\\\  & =0.4172.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(10.64<X<18.28)=0.4172.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6b42ee1-ded7-5130-90b2-9820cb2e4065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8694" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6b42ee1-ded7-5130-90b2-9820cb2e4065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8694" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365622} :answer-field/id #uuid "03543625-155c-5422-8957-6fdbb0c89c1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef524b79-6ad8-5245-9167-34b6ae9a0769" :question/math-academy-id "q-337164" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(5,{10}^{2}),$ compute $P(-18.4<X<16.7).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0075\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ | $\\,0.0071\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ | $\\,0.0094\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ | $\\,0.8621\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0075\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ | $\\,0.0071\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ | $\\,0.0094\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0129\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ | $\\,0.8621\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n\n$P(-18.4<X<16.7)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =5$ and $\\sigma =10.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-18.4<X<16.7) & =P(\\frac{-18.4-5}{10}<Z<\\frac{16.7-5}{10}) \\\\  & =P(-2.34<Z<1.17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/82e4ea3088176bbd9b03b7baa6aaa9389f21f9e9a286fa223d47bfae4252ccb6.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.34<Z<1.17) & =P(Z<1.17)-P(Z\\le -2.34) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.17)-P(Z\\le -2.34) \\\\  & =Φ(1.17)-Φ(-2.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.34) & =0.0096 \\\\ Φ(1.17) & =0.8790\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.34<Z<1.17) & =Φ(1.17)-Φ(-2.34) \\\\  & =0.8790-0.0096 \\\\  & =0.8694.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-18.4<X<16.7)=0.8694.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98bd56e8-77ca-553a-9c26-04feae0618dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c5edaab-2dc5-5b47-bb53-697b40115df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c5edaab-2dc5-5b47-bb53-697b40115df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365625} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddc47b99-fe84-5f34-82d0-28829ce29f0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eb7e5dc-0670-546b-bad1-c9cbf061a467" :question/math-academy-id "q-337167" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<13.5).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9505\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9599\\,$ | $\\,0.9608\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9678\\,$ | $\\,0.9686\\,$ | $\\,0.9693\\,$ |\n\n$P(X<13.5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =3$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<13.5) & =P(Z<\\frac{13.5-3}{6}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/e024a5ac6bdd86c88c6ed6cbe3a647281c63e6934c3830288fa01c1d51997e4a.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.75)=0.9599.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<13.5)=0.9599.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98bd56e8-77ca-553a-9c26-04feae0618dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35ea7e91-2d5e-53bd-94a0-544932d5d240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2514" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365628} {:answer/id #uuid "863dbeb1-d661-55cd-891f-f9e9ea43c8fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2546" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365629} {:answer/id #uuid "57530476-73aa-5718-8351-01f62053d98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2578" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365630} {:answer/id #uuid "50f239fe-3c7c-52dc-a221-d8b77cac1131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2236" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365631} {:answer/id #uuid "ca942e0b-78e2-50fe-983f-5da83b66adb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50f239fe-3c7c-52dc-a221-d8b77cac1131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2236" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365631} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d113680-9a34-5bda-a786-a3e6f5237b3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d522427b-b990-5c13-9935-456f89f8489e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94247" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-2,{3}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<-4.28).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1977\\,$ | $\\,0.1949\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2266\\,$ | $\\,0.2236\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.2578\\,$ | $\\,0.2546\\,$ | $\\,0.2514\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-2$ and $\\sigma =3.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-4.28) & =P(Z<\\frac{-4.28-(-2)}{3}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.76) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.76) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.76)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/56f9956d27f7527106869001273833c863d26053baf8c624c98d4ec2fb3c7f7b.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.76)=0.2236.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<-4.28)=0.2236.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82bebcca-0033-56d8-9eba-b5f8bf78d7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365635} {:answer/id #uuid "585d832c-0703-50fd-9166-1c0fb4a9171e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2812" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365636} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c14a9d-6908-5ac7-8987-305a0bce8053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2290" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365637} {:answer/id #uuid "ff545fbe-f8f9-5d67-9582-e0e6812e1de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2299" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365638} {:answer/id #uuid "19f00002-9178-550b-a945-acefb818cc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2892" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9c14a9d-6908-5ac7-8987-305a0bce8053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2290" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365637} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5c85319-0d0f-5bdd-aac1-9af104d593c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9b3ea12-1204-5e5a-a863-9796f78b4a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-94318" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(5,16),$ compute $P(0.64<X<3.64).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1020\\,$ | $\\,0.1003\\,$ | $\\,0.0985\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1210\\,$ | $\\,0.1190\\,$ | $\\,0.1170\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3336\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1020\\,$ | $\\,0.1003\\,$ | $\\,0.0985\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1210\\,$ | $\\,0.1190\\,$ | $\\,0.1170\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3336\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-5$ and $\\sigma =\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.64<X<3.64) & =P(\\frac{0.64-5}{4}<Z<\\frac{3.64-5}{4}) \\\\  & =P(-1.09<Z<-0.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/6c919bfe71b67ed94751cb53bd5f24e9e80e7979a28a8e28b123aac907d6b7e4.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.09<Z<-0.34) & =P(Z<-0.34)-P(Z\\le -1.09) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.34)-P(Z\\le -1.09) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.34)-Φ(-1.09)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.34) & =0.3669 \\\\ Φ(-1.09) & =0.1379\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.09<Z<-0.34) & =Φ(-0.34)-Φ(-1.09) \\\\  & =0.3669-0.1379 \\\\  & =0.2290.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(0.64<X<3.64)=0.2290.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec263f97-5b7f-55f5-9565-f7fed6c4262f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0214" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119355} {:answer/id #uuid "151009f4-ca1f-57d2-bb04-54e10c847b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0546" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119356} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee4df29-08bd-5f90-9bb8-b838e5cad9c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119357} {:answer/id #uuid "6d48d27e-de4d-5e3f-a723-dd51ce5407de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0386" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119358} {:answer/id #uuid "7249016a-882e-5ab7-b590-5d62e12fe313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0283" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7249016a-882e-5ab7-b590-5d62e12fe313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0283" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119359} :answer-field/id #uuid "740b597a-131b-5b9f-a68c-d7b3c31da21f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a6c56ff-5e80-58cf-bba5-4120eb643ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94319" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(2,25),$ compute $P(-0.65<X<-0.25).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ | $\\,0.2912\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3336\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =2$ and $\\sigma =\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.65<X<-0.25) & =P(\\frac{-0.65-2}{5}<Z<\\frac{-0.25-2}{5}) \\\\  & =P(-0.53<Z<-0.45)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/3d73cc4661eb9f68f636803918b3e55942e6538e4e70d13277d125386dad257e.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.53<Z<-0.45) & =P(Z<-0.45)-P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.45)-P(Z\\le -0.53) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.45)-Φ(-0.53)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.53) & =0.2981 \\\\ Φ(-0.45) & =0.3264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.53<Z<-0.45) & =Φ(-0.45)-Φ(-0.53) \\\\  & =0.3264-0.2981 \\\\  & =0.0283.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-0.65<X<-0.25)=0.0283.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af01940d-f4ef-53a2-b7e6-3ac45a9beb0c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0c3561f-eaeb-5a5b-a5ee-8b74fd06c6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3096" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365642} {:answer/id #uuid "148a0989-5bea-5de3-b582-47070e4dabb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365643} {:answer/id #uuid "cf619732-4323-577f-bfc6-4a512d266ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2834" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365644} {:answer/id #uuid "a336356b-0095-52f3-83e4-8a6d64a01c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365645} {:answer/id #uuid "516b3dba-c74a-5caa-98d3-cd87cfbd240c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2978" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0c3561f-eaeb-5a5b-a5ee-8b74fd06c6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3096" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365642} :answer-field/id #uuid "6596c70f-37a0-5144-a30f-a24837724971" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4dc9dc14-5df7-54d4-a1b0-b97576976f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-94327" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(4,{6}^{2}),$ compute $P(-3.2<X<2.86).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1357\\,$ | $\\,0.1335\\,$ | $\\,0.1314\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ | $\\,0.3520\\,$ | $\\,0.3483\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ | $\\,0.3897\\,$ | $\\,0.3859\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4325\\,$ | $\\,0.4286\\,$ | $\\,0.4247\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1357\\,$ | $\\,0.1335\\,$ | $\\,0.1314\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ | $\\,0.3520\\,$ | $\\,0.3483\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ | $\\,0.3897\\,$ | $\\,0.3859\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4325\\,$ | $\\,0.4286\\,$ | $\\,0.4247\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)=P(\\frac{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =4$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3.2<X<2.86) & =P(\\frac{-3.2-4}{6}<Z<\\frac{2.86-4}{6}) \\\\  & =P(-1.2<Z<-0.19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/1ed2c43f507a4d0ddd54126bcd3efaa2fa9b56c63ea908e8567e6959e3fc67bc.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.2<Z<-0.19) & =P(Z<-0.19)-P(Z\\le -1.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.19)-P(Z\\le -1.2) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.19)-Φ(-1.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.2) & =0.1151 \\\\ Φ(-0.19) & =0.4247\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.2<Z<-0.19) & =Φ(-0.19)-Φ(-1.2) \\\\  & =0.4247-0.1151 \\\\  & =0.3096.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-3.2<X<2.86)=0.3096.\n$$"}]}
